WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.040
So I hope you enjoyed the
lectures from Daniele.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:10.840
Now, I'm back.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.880
For the next three lectures,
I'll be your instructor.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.480
So let me first recap
where we are, and then I'm

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.240
going to introduce
the topic for today.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:24.160
And to do so, let's go
back to our usual roadmap

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.200
for this class.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.680
So far, what we have
been investigating

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:35.640
is the left-hand side branch
of this complicated roadmap.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.360
That is, we have been
looking at the methods that

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.420
solve the optimal control
problem in open loop form.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:46.160
That is what we are
trying to compute,

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.040
is the sequence of controls
parameterized by time,

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:55.760
that optimize a given cost
function given in additive form.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.720
And we'll be looking at the
two broad classes of methods

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:03.290
referred to as the indirect
methods and the direct methods.

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:06.730
These two classes of methods
are really complementary.

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:09.510
So in the case of
indirect methods,

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.730
the idea is to first derive
the necessary conditions

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:14.330
for optimality.

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:17.730
Basically, the generalization
of the gradient

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:21.010
equal to 0 condition from
non-linear optimization

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:24.850
to the setting of infinite
dimensional optimization, namely

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:26.530
optimal control.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:30.770
You saw that necessary
optimality condition boils down

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.410
to solving a 2
point boundary value

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:38.250
problem, that is a differential
equation with split boundary

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.250
conditions.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:43.690
And historically,
that's the first methods

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:47.130
that has been developed to
solve this class of problems.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.610
And then you also saw the
other approach referred

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:54.450
to as direct methods, where
the idea is to take the given

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:58.450
continuous time optimal
control problem, discretize it.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:00.690
And once you have
discretized the problem,

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.230
now you are back to the setting
of a nonlinear optimization,

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:07.410
and then use a nonlinear
solver of your choice

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:11.650
in order to solve that
nonlinear optimization problem.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:17.090
These days, particularly
for robotics, direct methods

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:18.970
are more popular.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:22.330
Thanks to advances in
algorithms, for example,

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:26.170
for nonlinear optimization,
advances in hardware is now

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:29.290
feasible to solve these
nonlinear optimization problems

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:32.330
on the edge, something
that was not, of course,

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.670
feasible both theoretically
and practically,

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:40.970
say back in the '60s or '70s
when indirect methods were

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:42.450
originally conceived.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.930
Let's say indirect
methods still have

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:48.250
some usefulness in the field.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.650
Sometimes they can give you
a closed form solution, which

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.090
of course, is the crown jewel.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:56.820
If you have the
explicit representation

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.820
of the optimal control
sequence, that's

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:03.140
great because it takes 0
time for implementation.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.780
And other times,
even though we're not

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:08.940
going to look at this
topic more deeply,

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:11.915
there are some synergies
between indirect optimal control

00:03:11.915 --> 00:03:13.040
and direct optimal control.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.340
So the two methodologies
can be hybridized.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:19.920
But rather than going more
deeply on this topic, today,

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.100
we're going to switch to the
right branch of this roadmap.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:28.380
Namely, we're going
to look at methods

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:32.020
that fall under the class of
closed loop optimal control

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:33.460
methodologies.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.000
We're solving the same problem.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.440
The problem doesn't change.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:44.700
What changes is the mathematical
object we are optimizing over.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:50.340
That is now our goal is to find
an optimal closed loop control

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.660
policy.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:58.220
We are no longer satisfied with
finding an open loop control

00:03:58.220 --> 00:03:59.580
sequence.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:06.500
Now, we want to find a control
law that maps for all possible

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:11.500
states that one can
achieve at time t.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.140
It gives us for
all possible states

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:17.120
that one can achieve at time
t, the optimal control u star.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:23.060
Again, as I said during
the first lecture,

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:28.460
the notion of open loop and
closed loop is a quite natural,

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:32.020
but sometimes may lead
to some confusions.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:37.580
Of course, the behavior of a
closed loop optimal control

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:42.620
policy is identical to one of
an open loop control sequence,

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:47.760
in case, the system follows
the nominal trajectory.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:53.220
That is the one induced by the
open loop control sequence.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:56.790
So you might say, why should we
bother about computing a closed

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:59.390
loop control policy?

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:01.470
Well, something that
is not accounted

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:04.570
for in our formulation, at
least in our basic formulation,

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:08.150
is the presence of disturbances
or model mismatches.

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:12.190
So even though mathematically
the problem that we are solving

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:14.030
doesn't involve any
disturbances or model

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:15.730
mismatches, in
practice, of course,

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:19.310
when you deploy these control
laws or optimal control

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:22.030
sequences, you're
controlling a system

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:25.070
whose underlying dynamical
model is different from the one

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:27.990
that you have used
for control design.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:31.590
An optimal control policy--
closed loop control policy

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:34.190
is a more robust object,
because the moment

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:39.230
that you go off the nominal
trajectory you know what to do.

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:41.470
While in this case,
you will blindly

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:45.470
apply the control that was
pre-computed for time stamp

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:48.750
t no matter what.

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.190
Is this part clear?

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:52.210
Sometimes it might lead
to some confusions.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:55.845
Yes?

00:05:55.845 --> 00:05:57.800
What is a [INAUDIBLE]?

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:58.860
U star?

00:05:58.860 --> 00:05:59.360
X.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:05:59.960
X?

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:02.200
The same.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.280
It marks the state, whatever
you are in the world.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.360
So you can think of UT
s as the right thing

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:12.860
to do if you are exactly along
the nominal state trajectory.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:15.960
But the moment that you
move farther from it,

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.320
you don't have any guarantee
anymore that that might

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:19.580
be the optimal thing to do.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:29.700
Now, to develop--
now, the question is--

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:34.440
OK, so it seems true that
closed loop control policies

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:38.520
are more robust and better
in general than open loop

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.400
control sequences.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.080
How do you solve for them?

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:44.440
So what we're
going to do next is

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:48.720
to discuss an algorithm referred
to as dynamic programming, that

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:54.240
is a very procedural way to
solve for optimal closed loop

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:56.800
control policies.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:00.000
Then you may say, why don't you
always use dynamic programming.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.840
Well, because
dynamic programming

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:05.903
is computationally
much more intensive.

00:07:05.903 --> 00:07:08.320
So the one hand, you have
something that is more powerful,

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:10.800
closed loop control
policies, but much

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:12.498
more computationally intensive.

00:07:12.498 --> 00:07:14.040
On the other hand,
you have something

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.560
that is computationally
much more

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.700
feasible, but less powerful.

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:23.040
That will lead us to MPC,
model predictive control,

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.380
where we try to get the
best of both worlds.

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:29.600
We try to reason in terms of a
closed loop control policies,

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:35.040
but in some sense with the
same computational footprint

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.260
of open loop control
methodologies.

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:40.880
That's what we will see
in a couple of weeks.

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:43.880
So does the plan make sense for
the next two or three weeks?

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:45.680
All right.

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:50.170
So then the task is how do
we find an algorithm that

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:54.690
allows us to solve for an
optimal closed loop control

00:07:54.690 --> 00:08:00.330
policy To do that, we're going
to derive the algorithm first

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:02.210
in a discrete time setting.

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:04.650
Next week, we will
extend the derivation

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:07.450
to the continuous time setting.

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:09.970
So for the discrete
time setting,

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:14.450
what we have is essentially
the same formulation

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:16.610
we saw during lecture 1.

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:19.810
But now, we are
discretizing time.

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:23.210
So time is no longer
a continuous variable,

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:25.090
is a discrete variable.

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:38.890
And each time instant is
denoted by tk or stage k.

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:41.970
The two terminologies
are equivalent.

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:44.750
Time tk could be
the discretization.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:49.070
It could be time 0, 0.1,
0.2, 0.3, or equivalently,

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:52.610
we could say stage 0
corresponds to time 0.

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:55.350
Stage k equal 1
corresponds to time 0.1.

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:57.550
Stage k equal 2
corresponds to time 0.2,

00:08:57.550 --> 00:08:59.170
and so on and so forth.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:05.090
Now, that we discretize time,
we have to discretize our update

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:05.790
equations.

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:08.330
That is the
mathematical formalism

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:11.370
that allows us to map the
current state and control

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:14.330
into the next state in the
original optimal control

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:18.850
formulation that was given by an
ordinary differential equation.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:24.290
Now, what we have is a discrete
time equation, which can either

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:26.370
be derived from the
continuous time equations

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:30.690
through discretization, or maybe
it just the most natural way

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:34.090
to define the dynamics
for your system.

00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:38.330
But in case you derive it from
the continuous time equations,

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:42.770
one very simple way to go from a
continuous time update equation

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:47.640
to discrete one is through
Euler discretization.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:51.260
So say that your dynamics are
given by x dot equals f of xut.

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:53.900
Dot, remember, is the
derivative with respect to time.

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:57.380
Then the derivative can be
approximated as xt plus delta

00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:01.120
t minus x of t over delta t.

00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:11.720
Which means that if you want
to reason in discrete time,

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:15.900
then you have the discrete
time update equation xt

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:29.340
plus delta t is equal to x
of t plus delta f of xut.

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:42.020
And then you define all of these
as your new dynamics equation

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:44.780
now discretized.

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:47.890
So f tilde will be the f here.

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:53.070
We have control
constraints as usual.

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:56.890
So they use-- now, also the
controls are discretized.

00:10:56.890 --> 00:11:00.710
You can take an action at stage
k, at stage k plus 1 and so on.

00:11:00.710 --> 00:11:03.350
The controls at
each stage k have

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:09.650
to belong to some set capital
U, that is also a function of x.

00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:15.310
So your control set can change
depending on where you are.

00:11:15.310 --> 00:11:20.230
Control constraints could be
you don't want to accelerate--

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:24.070
you cannot accelerate beyond
a certain acceleration level,

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:28.110
or you cannot impart thrust
beyond a certain thrust level

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:30.350
and so on.

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:31.690
Then we have the cost function.

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:33.630
So the cost function
structure is

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:38.510
the same as what we
had for the optimal--

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:40.630
continuous time optimal
control problem.

00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:42.450
It has an additive form.

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:47.710
So there is a term that sums
up the stage-wise contribution

00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:48.570
to the cost.

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:52.550
So this is the cost that you pay
at time k if you are in state xk

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:54.430
and you use control v or k.

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:57.650
Think about the fuel
that you're spending,

00:11:57.650 --> 00:12:02.430
or maybe how unhappy you are
about being a state x of k.

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:05.170
And h and xn is
the terminal cost,

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:10.010
how unhappy you are at the end
of your optimization horizon.

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:15.870
So after capital n time
steps, you're in state x of n.

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:17.790
So this is also the
discretized version

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:23.950
that we derive when we
discuss about direct methods.

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:25.990
So everything stays the same.

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:27.950
The main difference
is that now we

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:32.150
are interested in finding
optimal closed loop policies.

00:12:32.150 --> 00:12:35.710
That is, we want to
find the solution

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:39.310
in the form of a function pi.

00:12:39.310 --> 00:12:42.880
Star means optimal, the
optimization in general,

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:48.840
that maps whatever state you're
in and whatever time you're

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:53.280
in into the optimal thing to
do with respect to this cost

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:53.900
function.

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:01.360
Before it would have been-- when
we were talking about open loop

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:05.240
controls, the object
we were optimizing over

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:07.580
was simply the
sequence of u0 star,

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:11.680
u1 star, and so on and so forth.

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:13.020
Not a function of the state.

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:16.920
Now, we want to find it as
a function of the state.

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:20.860
It might sound as a
simple terminology.

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:24.320
This has a very
deep implications,

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:27.902
as I explained before.

00:13:27.902 --> 00:13:28.860
This should make sense.

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:29.920
What do you think?

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:30.920
All right.

00:13:30.920 --> 00:13:34.960
So now the question
is, how do we

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:41.800
find an algorithm to find such
an optimal closed loop policy?

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:43.680
And the key to
crack the problem is

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:47.380
to recognize that this
problem does have structure,

00:13:47.380 --> 00:13:49.320
and the structure
in this problem

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:52.960
can be used to derive a fairly
efficient algorithm referred

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:54.760
to as dynamic programming.

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:57.040
And the structure
that this problem has

00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:00.960
is referred to as the
principle of optimality,

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:07.600
which is the consequence
of using an additive cost

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:10.360
as our cost function.

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:12.780
If instead of a summation,
you use something else,

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:15.560
the tangent of the
sum of the stuff,

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:17.680
that doesn't work anymore.

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:21.160
So here, you see how our
formulation is a good compromise

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:23.360
between being
relatively general,

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:25.840
but also having enough
structure for us

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:29.880
to derive a usable,
actionable algorithms.

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:31.540
So what is the
principle of optimality?

00:14:31.540 --> 00:14:37.220
The principle of optimality
is a fairly simple concept.

00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:40.890
In my experience, the first
time that you hear about it,

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:42.650
you think it's obvious.

00:14:42.650 --> 00:14:45.790
If you think more about
it, you get a bit confused,

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:48.810
but then after a while,
it becomes obvious again.

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:51.250
So let me state the
principle of optimality.

00:14:51.250 --> 00:14:53.290
Principle of
optimality essentially

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:59.490
says that if you have a
multi-step optimal control

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:05.130
problem, so you're optimizing
operations over multiple steps,

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:08.850
the details of an
optimal policy are

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:11.050
optimal for the
truncated problem.

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:14.050
I'll explain better what I mean.

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:18.290
Let me first show
a simple statement

00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:22.810
of the principle of
optimality and a proof of it.

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:27.490
So let's assume that we want
to solve an optimal control

00:15:27.490 --> 00:15:41.780
problem that is abstractly
represented as finding

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:44.160
some path in some space.

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:54.180
So suppose that you have an
initial condition referred

00:15:54.180 --> 00:16:01.020
to as a and you have a final
condition referred to as e.

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:03.620
And you want to go from a to e.

00:16:03.620 --> 00:16:09.460
And to do so, you have to
enact a number of decisions.

00:16:09.460 --> 00:16:10.915
You thrust in one direction.

00:16:10.915 --> 00:16:13.040
And after a while, you
thrust in another direction.

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:14.020
You keep going.

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:18.420
And then you get to
your final condition e.

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:25.020
So let's assume that the
optimal path stemming

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:32.100
from the optimal selection of
controls first leads you to b.

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:36.580
And then through a number
of additional decisions

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:40.280
leads to e through this path.

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:53.744
So this piece here
is the realization

00:16:53.744 --> 00:17:05.599
of many additional
controls, control inputs.

00:17:14.579 --> 00:17:18.980
Now, let's call the cost
of transitioning from a

00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:25.260
to b along this path as jab.

00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:29.540
And let's call the cost of
transitioning from b to e

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:32.620
along this specific path as jbe.

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:40.790
So according to our
formulation, what

00:17:40.790 --> 00:17:45.230
is the total cost for going
from a to e along what

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:47.435
I claim is an optimal path?

00:17:47.435 --> 00:17:49.070
The cost of [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:50.055
A to E.

00:17:50.055 --> 00:17:50.890
That is equal too.

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:52.390
That's right, because
we're assuming

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:54.470
that we have an additive cost.

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:58.630
So the optimal
cost j from a to e

00:17:58.630 --> 00:18:05.270
is simply the sum
of jab plus jbe.

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:08.390
And that stems from the
fact that we formulated

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:10.070
the optimal control
problem with respect

00:18:10.070 --> 00:18:14.630
to a cost function
that is additive.

00:18:14.630 --> 00:18:16.670
So what the principle
of optimality is saying

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:22.790
is that if the red
highlighted path a--

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:27.510
first, we go to b and then we
go to e along this red path,

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:35.030
so if this path is optimal to go
from a to e, then the tail of it

00:18:35.030 --> 00:18:37.970
is optimal for the
truncated problem.

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:43.390
So if I were to reformulate the
problem, now starting from b.

00:18:43.390 --> 00:18:48.030
So now I should say, well,
that sounds obvious, but let's

00:18:48.030 --> 00:18:48.830
prove it.

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:52.030
So what I'm saying is
that if a to b and then

00:18:52.030 --> 00:18:56.430
b to e along the red highlighted
path is the indeed optimal path

00:18:56.430 --> 00:19:01.470
to go from a to e, then
the tail of this path,

00:19:01.470 --> 00:19:08.110
meaning the path that starts
at b and brings us to e,

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:11.650
is optimal for the
problem when started at b.

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:22.430
And let's prove this
statement by contradiction.

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:28.710
So proving by contradiction
means that we negate the claim,

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:31.110
and we'll see that we'll
come up with a contradiction

00:19:31.110 --> 00:19:33.880
with our hypothesis,
which in turn proves

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:36.920
that the claim has to be true.

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:40.040
That is suppose by contradiction
that what I'm saying

00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:41.560
is not true.

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:45.200
That is for the problem
whereby we start at b

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:50.560
and we want to get to e
in the most optimal way,

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:53.360
there is indeed another
strategy whereby we first

00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:56.760
go to some state,
let's call it c,

00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:59.380
and then we follow a
path that goes to e.

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:09.480
If this total path is indeed the
optimal one for the problem that

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:22.440
starts at b, it means that j b
going to c first and going to e,

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:30.080
let's call this cost
jbce, is less than jbe.

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:34.130
Jbe is the cost of the
red highlighted path.

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:38.010
Jbce is the cost
of going to e first

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:45.010
going through the state c, the
cost of this different path.

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:47.570
Well, that was true.

00:20:47.570 --> 00:20:53.170
It means that I would have found
a better path to go from a to e.

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:55.530
Simply the one that
goes from a to b first,

00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:58.210
then to c, and then to e.

00:20:58.210 --> 00:21:00.690
Because of this, the
cost of this path

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:08.490
would now be jab
plus jbce, which

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:17.670
will be strictly less than jab
plus a jbe because of this.

00:21:20.530 --> 00:21:24.970
Which means that I would
have found a better

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:28.930
path to go from a to e,
contradicting the claim--

00:21:28.930 --> 00:21:32.010
contradicting the
assumption that the a, b, e

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:34.010
is the optimal path
to go from a to e.

00:21:34.010 --> 00:21:35.510
So we have achieved
a contradiction,

00:21:35.510 --> 00:21:42.570
which proves our claim that
if you have an optimal control

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:45.410
sequence, the tail
of such a sequence

00:21:45.410 --> 00:21:47.790
is optimal for the
truncated problem.

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:54.250
Does it make sense?

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:55.370
More or less.

00:21:55.370 --> 00:21:57.310
Spend a little time
to think about it.

00:21:57.310 --> 00:22:02.970
Even though this principle
seems quite harmless,

00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:05.190
actually, it has
profound implications.

00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:05.750
Yeah?

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:08.110
Is it just the tail
of your sequence

00:22:08.110 --> 00:22:13.150
that's not true for any subset
of as say you have a, b, c, e?

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:14.470
It has to be put in tail.

00:22:14.470 --> 00:22:15.530
Just the tail?

00:22:15.530 --> 00:22:16.330
Yes.

00:22:16.330 --> 00:22:19.270
And then-- yeah, let me say,
for now, it's just the tail.

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:19.770
Yes.

00:22:25.930 --> 00:22:29.420
So let me reformulate now
the principle of optimality

00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:33.380
in the same formalism
that I discussed

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:36.562
before to state the discrete
time optimal control problem.

00:22:36.562 --> 00:22:38.020
So what the principle
of optimality

00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:45.260
is saying is that if u0 star,
u1 star, u star N minus 1

00:22:45.260 --> 00:22:49.340
is an optimal control
sequence, which together with x

00:22:49.340 --> 00:22:52.780
star 0 determines the
corresponding state sequence

00:22:52.780 --> 00:22:58.300
x star 0, x1 star, x star
n and so on, consider then

00:22:58.300 --> 00:23:03.020
the subproblem whereby we are
at state x star k at time k

00:23:03.020 --> 00:23:08.020
and we wish to minimize the
truncated problem, whereby

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:16.140
we start at x star k and we only
look at the cost after time k.

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:19.360
Then the truncated
optimal sequence,

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:24.100
so the sequence whereby we
now start at the uk star,

00:23:24.100 --> 00:23:26.320
is optimal for the subproblem.

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:32.460
A more mnemonic way to
remember this condition

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:36.760
is the tail of optimal-- the
tail of optimal sequence.

00:23:36.760 --> 00:23:38.940
The tail optimal
sequences are optimal

00:23:38.940 --> 00:23:40.440
for the tail subproblems.

00:23:48.420 --> 00:23:50.160
So you say, who cares?

00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:52.400
We'll see in a second why
we do care about this.

00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:53.100
Yes?

00:23:53.100 --> 00:23:55.220
This formulation is
almost a little bit

00:23:55.220 --> 00:23:58.020
like triangle inequality
for Jensen's inequality

00:23:58.020 --> 00:24:01.380
or really similar ideas
in different packaging

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:03.420
or [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:05.340
I think it's even
more basic idea

00:24:05.340 --> 00:24:08.580
that follows from the
additive assumption

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:09.760
that we make in the cost.

00:24:09.760 --> 00:24:10.940
You see it here.

00:24:10.940 --> 00:24:18.060
The only reason why
we can make this claim

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:22.160
is because we could assume
additivity in the cost.

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:24.353
By the way, it's in
a weaker condition,

00:24:24.353 --> 00:24:26.020
because the only thing
that we're saying

00:24:26.020 --> 00:24:27.930
is that we need to
have additivity,

00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:32.350
no matter which weird space
your optimization could be over.

00:24:32.350 --> 00:24:36.190
So that's why this
property is so general.

00:24:36.190 --> 00:24:41.517
So the next question is,
why do we care about this?

00:24:41.517 --> 00:24:43.350
So let me give you a
little bit of intuition

00:24:43.350 --> 00:24:46.110
about why we care about this.

00:24:46.110 --> 00:24:49.710
So suppose that, again, we
are in a setting whereby we

00:24:49.710 --> 00:24:52.270
want to find an optimal path.

00:24:52.270 --> 00:24:55.110
So the final destination is f.

00:24:55.110 --> 00:24:59.990
And suppose that an oracle
is giving you the solution

00:24:59.990 --> 00:25:05.920
to go optimally from a point
e to f with cost j star ef.

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:08.430
And it's giving you
this optimal solution

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:13.590
to go from d to f
with cost j star df.

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:15.590
And it's giving you
the optimal solution

00:25:15.590 --> 00:25:20.870
to go from c to f
with cost j, star cf

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:24.640
in oracle, the structure that's
giving you this information.

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:28.800
And these optimal paths may be
the result of the application

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:32.480
of 324 control inputs.

00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:34.320
So there is a lot of
computation that went

00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:38.200
into computing these paths.

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:39.960
And now, it's a midterm.

00:25:39.960 --> 00:25:44.080
And you have three minutes
to answer this question.

00:25:44.080 --> 00:25:48.880
So I'm giving you now the
problem of going from a b to f.

00:25:48.880 --> 00:25:53.180
And from b, you can go
to only three states.

00:25:53.180 --> 00:25:56.920
You can go to c directly
with cost at jbc,

00:25:56.920 --> 00:25:59.760
you can go to d
directly with cost jbd,

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:03.800
or you can go to e
directly with cost jbe.

00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:07.760
And then I ask you and, of
course, I give you this cost.

00:26:07.760 --> 00:26:10.960
And then I ask you to
compute the optimal path

00:26:10.960 --> 00:26:15.040
to go from b to f.

00:26:15.040 --> 00:26:18.000
So if you knew nothing about
the principle of optimality,

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:22.560
what you would need
to do is to consider

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:25.520
each one of these
options, and then

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:31.308
resolve for all possible control
combinations for the tail path.

00:26:31.308 --> 00:26:33.600
You wouldn't have a way to
actually use the information

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:35.440
that are given to you.

00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:37.980
But now, you know about the
principle of optimality.

00:26:37.980 --> 00:26:40.120
And the principle
optimality is telling you

00:26:40.120 --> 00:26:44.880
that if the arc bc is
the initial segment

00:26:44.880 --> 00:26:48.760
of the optimal path
from b to f, then

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:52.280
c to f is the terminal
segment of this path.

00:26:52.280 --> 00:26:53.760
That is, there is
no reason for you

00:26:53.760 --> 00:26:58.240
to consider any further
combinations of possible control

00:26:58.240 --> 00:26:59.980
inputs for the
tails of problems,

00:26:59.980 --> 00:27:05.600
because you know that the tail
of an optimal control problem--

00:27:05.600 --> 00:27:09.140
the tail of an optimal
control sequence

00:27:09.140 --> 00:27:11.400
is optimal for the tail problem.

00:27:11.400 --> 00:27:13.360
So there is no
additional optimality

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:15.240
you can gain for
further optimizing

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:17.320
the truncated problem.

00:27:17.320 --> 00:27:22.250
So what that means is that
instead of a brute force solving

00:27:22.250 --> 00:27:25.290
the entire problem
from the beginning,

00:27:25.290 --> 00:27:28.590
you can just do
simple concatenation.

00:27:28.590 --> 00:27:32.570
So you just look
at three options.

00:27:32.570 --> 00:27:34.730
Concatenating the
initial segment

00:27:34.730 --> 00:27:37.410
with the optimal solution
to the tail problem, one,

00:27:37.410 --> 00:27:41.810
second, possible initial segment
with the optimal solution

00:27:41.810 --> 00:27:45.032
to the second tail
problem, and third segment

00:27:45.032 --> 00:27:46.990
with the optimal solution
to the third problem.

00:27:46.990 --> 00:27:50.210
So you only have to look at
three choices, as opposed

00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:53.850
to recompute everything
from scratch.

00:27:53.850 --> 00:27:57.690
So that's the part where things
get a bit more interesting

00:27:57.690 --> 00:28:00.650
and things might get
slightly confusing,

00:28:00.650 --> 00:28:03.490
but I hope that this
example clarifies it.

00:28:03.490 --> 00:28:05.230
So let's suppose,
for example-- yeah?

00:28:05.230 --> 00:28:08.370
You say that those three
tail segments are all--

00:28:08.370 --> 00:28:09.910
they're all the same costs.

00:28:09.910 --> 00:28:11.990
No, they don't need
to be the same cost.

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:14.130
Actually, in general,
they have different cost.

00:28:14.130 --> 00:28:16.930
But what I'm saying is
that each one of them

00:28:16.930 --> 00:28:20.750
is optimal for the particular
subproblem where I start from e,

00:28:20.750 --> 00:28:23.350
I start from d or for c.

00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:29.410
For example, would
it be very obvious--

00:28:29.410 --> 00:28:33.210
just because looking
at the image,

00:28:33.210 --> 00:28:36.610
I'm struggling to
understand like why--

00:28:36.610 --> 00:28:39.090
if we didn't know the
principle of optimality,

00:28:39.090 --> 00:28:41.810
if we started with the
cost function from e

00:28:41.810 --> 00:28:44.970
to c, why we would choose
that from d to f and not just

00:28:44.970 --> 00:28:47.290
from c to f.

00:28:47.290 --> 00:28:48.930
Could you repeat the last part?

00:28:48.930 --> 00:28:52.610
I'm just a little confused
why we would decide--

00:28:52.610 --> 00:28:57.610
if we were going from b to
c, wouldn't the obvious thing

00:28:57.610 --> 00:28:59.130
be to go from c to f?

00:28:59.130 --> 00:29:04.730
Why look at d to f, for example,
if you're already doing b to c?

00:29:04.730 --> 00:29:07.370
So first of all, there
might be multiple paths

00:29:07.370 --> 00:29:11.250
to go from c to f or from
d to f, or from e to f.

00:29:11.250 --> 00:29:15.130
And here, I'm just
showing the optimal one.

00:29:15.130 --> 00:29:18.970
If you go from b
to c, yes, sure,

00:29:18.970 --> 00:29:21.672
it's obvious a little
bit, considering principle

00:29:21.672 --> 00:29:23.380
of optimality, that
the right thing to do

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:26.260
is to go through
the optimal path.

00:29:26.260 --> 00:29:29.540
But what is not obvious,
since each of these costs

00:29:29.540 --> 00:29:31.620
are different and
each of these costs

00:29:31.620 --> 00:29:35.140
are different, starting from b,
which one of the three options

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:37.220
you should choose?

00:29:37.220 --> 00:29:38.900
That's a non-obvious part.

00:29:38.900 --> 00:29:43.060
Yes, if you are
committed to go to c,

00:29:43.060 --> 00:29:46.740
then yes, it's obvious that
you would need-- but again,

00:29:46.740 --> 00:29:49.060
it's obvious because
I just explained

00:29:49.060 --> 00:29:51.677
the principle of optimality.

00:29:51.677 --> 00:29:53.260
That's why the
principle of optimality

00:29:53.260 --> 00:29:55.860
is a bit sketchy as a
concept, because it's

00:29:55.860 --> 00:29:59.380
so obvious that sometimes people
really fail to fully appreciate

00:29:59.380 --> 00:30:00.780
its depth.

00:30:00.780 --> 00:30:05.220
So yes, it's obvious, but there
is some cleverness into it.

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:08.540
I'm going to give you another
example to hopefully make

00:30:08.540 --> 00:30:09.873
things even clearer.

00:30:09.873 --> 00:30:11.540
But anyway, the main
point of this slide

00:30:11.540 --> 00:30:14.900
is to show how, with the
principle of optimality,

00:30:14.900 --> 00:30:17.910
we can reuse computation
that has already

00:30:17.910 --> 00:30:22.510
been done, either by an
oracle or, as we will see,

00:30:22.510 --> 00:30:26.470
by us through a
recursive algorithm,

00:30:26.470 --> 00:30:29.270
so that you minimize the
amount of computation

00:30:29.270 --> 00:30:30.490
that you do overall.

00:30:33.230 --> 00:30:36.950
So bottom line is that applying
the principle of optimality

00:30:36.950 --> 00:30:39.750
allows you to compare
the concatenations

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:42.550
of immediate decisions
and optimal decisions

00:30:42.550 --> 00:30:46.110
in order to compute
optimal control

00:30:46.110 --> 00:30:48.510
sequence for an
optimal problem that

00:30:48.510 --> 00:30:51.250
has been extended by one step.

00:30:54.990 --> 00:30:59.270
Then the next question is,
who is solving those state

00:30:59.270 --> 00:31:01.590
problems for you?

00:31:01.590 --> 00:31:04.550
In general, you
don't have an oracle.

00:31:04.550 --> 00:31:12.470
So that's where the somewhat
anointed and non-intuitive idea

00:31:12.470 --> 00:31:14.510
of dynamic programming comes in.

00:31:14.510 --> 00:31:17.870
You solve the problem
backward in time,

00:31:17.870 --> 00:31:21.730
even though, of course,
time moves forward in time.

00:31:21.730 --> 00:31:24.950
So you deploy your policy
going forward in time.

00:31:24.950 --> 00:31:29.550
The problem is solved
backward in time.

00:31:29.550 --> 00:31:33.190
You start from the end,
simply because at the end,

00:31:33.190 --> 00:31:36.935
the optimal solution is obvious.

00:31:36.935 --> 00:31:37.810
There is no solution.

00:31:37.810 --> 00:31:38.602
You are at the end.

00:31:38.602 --> 00:31:40.770
So there is nothing
you should do.

00:31:40.770 --> 00:31:42.450
And the cost is also obvious.

00:31:42.450 --> 00:31:44.870
It's just a terminal cost.

00:31:44.870 --> 00:31:49.350
That means that you
know terminal-- you

00:31:49.350 --> 00:31:53.310
know the optimal solution
at the terminal stage.

00:31:53.310 --> 00:31:56.910
But now, we have a procedure to
go one step at a time backward

00:31:56.910 --> 00:32:02.990
by just concatenating immediate
costs with the solution

00:32:02.990 --> 00:32:04.370
to the tail problems.

00:32:04.370 --> 00:32:08.028
The first time you do it, you
just have the terminal cost.

00:32:08.028 --> 00:32:10.070
And this is the boundary
conditions given to you.

00:32:10.070 --> 00:32:13.860
And then you start applying
it recursively, this idea.

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:15.360
And for the rest
of the lecture, I'm

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:19.240
going to give you a bit more
intuition about this procedure,

00:32:19.240 --> 00:32:21.520
which is extremely general.

00:32:21.520 --> 00:32:24.840
But let me start with
one example, where

00:32:24.840 --> 00:32:29.760
we're going to do these
backward recursions by hand.

00:32:29.760 --> 00:32:32.680
And this example
is more mechanical,

00:32:32.680 --> 00:32:34.640
just to show the
mechanics of it.

00:32:34.640 --> 00:32:36.400
But then later on,
we're also going

00:32:36.400 --> 00:32:39.600
to explore how much
computational savings

00:32:39.600 --> 00:32:41.240
this buys us.

00:32:41.240 --> 00:32:44.280
So now, we have a
shortest path problem

00:32:44.280 --> 00:32:46.480
that is a bit more concrete.

00:32:46.480 --> 00:32:51.240
We want to start from a
and we want to go to h.

00:32:51.240 --> 00:32:53.840
And the transitions
that are possible

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:55.640
are denoted by
these arrows, which

00:32:55.640 --> 00:33:00.520
means that if you are at b,
you can move right to state

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:04.520
c with cost equal to 9.

00:33:04.520 --> 00:33:09.520
Or for example, if you're at
c, you can move only right--

00:33:09.520 --> 00:33:12.640
actually, no, you can move
right to f with cost 3

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:16.630
or you can move
upward with cost 5.

00:33:16.630 --> 00:33:25.800
So the question is, what is the
best path to go from a to h?

00:33:25.800 --> 00:33:28.040
If you knew nothing about
dynamic programming, what

00:33:28.040 --> 00:33:33.160
you would do is to compute
all possible combinations.

00:33:33.160 --> 00:33:38.380
So you go down, you
go right, you go up.

00:33:38.380 --> 00:33:39.940
That's one possible sequence.

00:33:39.940 --> 00:33:40.915
You go right.

00:33:40.915 --> 00:33:42.540
That's another branch
of your sequence.

00:33:42.540 --> 00:33:44.915
You consider all possible
combinations that are feasible,

00:33:44.915 --> 00:33:47.240
and then you pick the
one with minimum cost.

00:33:47.240 --> 00:33:50.600
Now, actually, we're going to
use the principle of optimality

00:33:50.600 --> 00:33:54.400
to be a bit more principled
in how we do search.

00:33:54.400 --> 00:33:58.600
Specifically, we
start from the back.

00:33:58.600 --> 00:34:06.160
So what is the cost
for the shortest path

00:34:06.160 --> 00:34:13.290
problem whereby we start at h?

00:34:13.290 --> 00:34:16.530
So that's the tail problem
where we start at h.

00:34:16.530 --> 00:34:19.250
So what is the cost?

00:34:19.250 --> 00:34:21.889
We have h and we
want to go to h.

00:34:21.889 --> 00:34:22.449
0.

00:34:22.449 --> 00:34:23.449
Great.

00:34:23.449 --> 00:34:27.130
So we know actually the
solution to this k problem.

00:34:27.130 --> 00:34:34.389
So now, let's go one step
backward, for example, here, g.

00:34:37.170 --> 00:34:39.190
So what is the cost of g?

00:34:39.190 --> 00:34:52.050
So the cost of g is equal to the
immediate cost 2 to go upward.

00:34:52.050 --> 00:34:53.510
And then I had the tail problem.

00:34:53.510 --> 00:34:56.002
It starts at h.

00:34:56.002 --> 00:34:57.710
I don't have to resolve
for that problem.

00:34:57.710 --> 00:34:59.570
I already have the solution.

00:34:59.570 --> 00:35:02.290
So this would be the 2 plus gh.

00:35:02.290 --> 00:35:04.650
But I have already solved for h.

00:35:04.650 --> 00:35:06.450
H is equal to 0.

00:35:06.450 --> 00:35:10.820
So the optimal cost is 2.

00:35:10.820 --> 00:35:16.340
So let's assume that
now we are at f.

00:35:16.340 --> 00:35:20.260
So what is the optimal
cost to go from f to h?

00:35:20.260 --> 00:35:23.540
Well, it's the
immediate cost, which

00:35:23.540 --> 00:35:33.940
is 3, plus the cost of the k
problem, which is also referred

00:35:33.940 --> 00:35:40.780
to as cost to go, which is
j of g, which is equal to 2.

00:35:40.780 --> 00:35:43.300
So this is equal to 5.

00:35:43.300 --> 00:35:45.400
And then we go one
step backwards.

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:51.340
So now, we have a j of e.

00:35:51.340 --> 00:35:52.980
So e is a kind of
an interesting case

00:35:52.980 --> 00:35:55.740
because we have two options.

00:35:55.740 --> 00:36:00.020
So remember, the more
abstract figure that I

00:36:00.020 --> 00:36:02.420
had before, I have two options.

00:36:02.420 --> 00:36:09.260
I can either go right, I have
an immediate cost equal to 8,

00:36:09.260 --> 00:36:12.300
and then I transition
directly into h.

00:36:12.300 --> 00:36:18.680
So the cost of this path
would be 8 plus a j of h.

00:36:22.540 --> 00:36:28.020
But then I can also go down in
current cost 2 and transition

00:36:28.020 --> 00:36:31.580
into state f.

00:36:31.580 --> 00:36:34.340
But I don't have to recompute
what is the optimal thing

00:36:34.340 --> 00:36:35.420
to do from f.

00:36:35.420 --> 00:36:36.920
I already have
computed it before.

00:36:36.920 --> 00:36:40.580
So this will be 2 plus j of f.

00:36:40.580 --> 00:36:42.020
This is correct.

00:36:42.020 --> 00:36:44.860
So a plus gh, 2 plus gf.

00:36:44.860 --> 00:36:47.360
So gh, already computed.

00:36:47.360 --> 00:36:49.580
So this is equal to 0.

00:36:49.580 --> 00:36:51.120
Gf, already computed.

00:36:51.120 --> 00:36:52.900
This is equal to 5.

00:36:52.900 --> 00:36:55.120
So now, I just pick the
minimum between two.

00:36:55.120 --> 00:36:58.620
So this is equal--
the first path is 8.

00:36:58.620 --> 00:37:00.820
Second path is 7.

00:37:00.820 --> 00:37:08.630
So I pick the path that goes
to f and the cost will be 7.

00:37:08.630 --> 00:37:11.550
Then I go one step backwards.

00:37:11.550 --> 00:37:12.850
So I have j of d.

00:37:15.950 --> 00:37:19.210
So this is equal to 3 plus ge.

00:37:22.550 --> 00:37:24.250
Ge, already computed.

00:37:24.250 --> 00:37:26.830
So this is equal to 10.

00:37:26.830 --> 00:37:29.430
Then I have c.

00:37:29.430 --> 00:37:36.630
So j of c is equal to
the minimum between 3

00:37:36.630 --> 00:37:45.810
plus jf and 5 plus jd.

00:37:49.590 --> 00:37:52.790
No need to recompute jd.

00:37:52.790 --> 00:37:55.550
I can recycle the
fact that I already

00:37:55.550 --> 00:37:58.470
solved for the tail problems.

00:37:58.470 --> 00:38:01.430
Jd is equal to 10.

00:38:01.430 --> 00:38:05.110
Jf is equal to 5.

00:38:05.110 --> 00:38:07.630
So 3 plus 5, 8, 15.

00:38:07.630 --> 00:38:09.930
So the optimal cost is 8.

00:38:17.270 --> 00:38:30.070
Yes, then jb is
equal to 9 plus jc.

00:38:30.070 --> 00:38:34.070
So this is equal to 17.

00:38:34.070 --> 00:38:40.030
And finally, I hit my
initial condition a.

00:38:40.030 --> 00:38:41.050
So I have two options.

00:38:41.050 --> 00:38:54.330
I can either go right, 8 plus
jd, or I can go down 5 plus jb.

00:38:57.830 --> 00:39:02.270
Jd already computed
is equal to 10.

00:39:02.270 --> 00:39:07.100
Jb already computed equal to 17.

00:39:07.100 --> 00:39:08.580
10 plus 8 wins.

00:39:08.580 --> 00:39:10.260
So optimal cost equal to 18.

00:39:13.720 --> 00:39:19.480
And now, I can backtrack
what the minimizer was

00:39:19.480 --> 00:39:23.720
at every stage of optimization.

00:39:23.720 --> 00:39:29.900
So from a, we selected
this one as a minimizer.

00:39:29.900 --> 00:39:33.520
So from a, we want to go to d.

00:39:33.520 --> 00:39:36.740
From d, we have
only one options.

00:39:36.740 --> 00:39:38.520
We go to e.

00:39:38.520 --> 00:39:42.240
And from e, we go the minimizer.

00:39:42.240 --> 00:39:45.540
We go to f from f to
g and from g to h.

00:39:51.180 --> 00:39:51.680
Yeah?

00:39:51.680 --> 00:39:54.000
Are we [INAUDIBLE]?

00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:56.680
Like, for instance, how
do we know that from e

00:39:56.680 --> 00:39:58.880
to h with several [INAUDIBLE]?

00:39:58.880 --> 00:40:01.533
And there is a difference
study between other stuff

00:40:01.533 --> 00:40:02.200
and [INAUDIBLE]?

00:40:02.200 --> 00:40:03.180
Yeah, great question.

00:40:03.180 --> 00:40:05.820
So in order to run
dynamic programming,

00:40:05.820 --> 00:40:11.240
you have to put your problem
in the mathematical formalism

00:40:11.240 --> 00:40:12.760
that I explained before.

00:40:12.760 --> 00:40:15.520
So here, we will need to
define states and time

00:40:15.520 --> 00:40:20.160
steps in terms of
how many steps before

00:40:20.160 --> 00:40:24.580
we hit the final condition.

00:40:24.580 --> 00:40:26.800
And there are ways to actually
formulate the shortest

00:40:26.800 --> 00:40:28.180
paths in such a way.

00:40:28.180 --> 00:40:32.000
So the k, the index, will
be the number of steps

00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:36.160
to get to your destination h.

00:40:36.160 --> 00:40:39.200
So in other words, we want to--

00:40:39.200 --> 00:40:41.800
that's actually the tricky
part of dynamic programming.

00:40:41.800 --> 00:40:46.080
That's where dynamic
programming gets tricky.

00:40:46.080 --> 00:40:47.840
I'll give it to you in a second.

00:40:47.840 --> 00:40:50.160
Dynamic programming gets
tricky in the following way.

00:40:50.160 --> 00:40:55.520
Once your problem is defined
in the canonical way,

00:40:55.520 --> 00:41:00.280
so you have discrete time
equation for your dynamics,

00:41:00.280 --> 00:41:04.050
and then you have an additive
cost for your optimization

00:41:04.050 --> 00:41:08.210
problem, then at
least conceptually,

00:41:08.210 --> 00:41:09.790
the algorithm is trivial.

00:41:12.370 --> 00:41:16.690
You solve a recursive algorithm
where you start from a boundary

00:41:16.690 --> 00:41:20.690
condition whereby you
set the optimal solution

00:41:20.690 --> 00:41:21.670
to the tail problem.

00:41:21.670 --> 00:41:24.210
That is, the problem that
starts at the final condition

00:41:24.210 --> 00:41:25.890
as the terminal cost.

00:41:25.890 --> 00:41:29.170
And then you iterate
one step backward

00:41:29.170 --> 00:41:32.690
until you reach the
initial state x of 0.

00:41:35.330 --> 00:41:41.130
At each iteration level
by the time step k,

00:41:41.130 --> 00:41:42.890
you solve the
minimization problem

00:41:42.890 --> 00:41:45.130
whereby you're trying to
solve for a given state

00:41:45.130 --> 00:41:48.770
x of k the optimal
control, the best

00:41:48.770 --> 00:41:52.710
compromise between immediate
cost and so-called cost to go,

00:41:52.710 --> 00:41:57.570
so the cost of the
truncated problem.

00:41:57.570 --> 00:41:59.690
And as you can tell, I
mean, this is very simple

00:41:59.690 --> 00:42:04.500
to implement conceptually.

00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:06.420
But there are a few caveats.

00:42:06.420 --> 00:42:12.780
First of all, there are a number
of problems that at first look,

00:42:12.780 --> 00:42:18.140
they seem to be completely
different from the way

00:42:18.140 --> 00:42:20.460
that I'm stating
the problem here.

00:42:20.460 --> 00:42:22.940
And that's where the cleverness
of the designer goes.

00:42:22.940 --> 00:42:26.420
You map those problems
into this formalism.

00:42:26.420 --> 00:42:29.960
Even though I haven't shown you,
but I think we have a homework.

00:42:29.960 --> 00:42:33.780
Otherwise, I will put
a reference on Canvas.

00:42:33.780 --> 00:42:36.780
There is a way to map the
shortest path problems exactly

00:42:36.780 --> 00:42:39.700
this formalism.

00:42:39.700 --> 00:42:41.440
So that's the first caveat.

00:42:41.440 --> 00:42:42.660
So there is some work.

00:42:42.660 --> 00:42:45.500
And we'll see a
couple of examples

00:42:45.500 --> 00:42:49.712
whereby you take a problem,
for example, in DNA sequencing,

00:42:49.712 --> 00:42:51.420
that the first value
seems it has nothing

00:42:51.420 --> 00:42:52.720
to do with the programming.

00:42:52.720 --> 00:42:54.100
You can put in this formalism.

00:42:54.100 --> 00:42:57.760
And once you have the problem
formulating this formalism,

00:42:57.760 --> 00:42:59.240
you can just run the algorithm.

00:42:59.240 --> 00:43:04.600
So you say OK, then it's
more of a modeling exercise.

00:43:04.600 --> 00:43:06.540
No, it's also an
algorithmic exercise,

00:43:06.540 --> 00:43:11.540
because even though
dynamic programming allows

00:43:11.540 --> 00:43:14.860
you to decrease substantially
the amount of computations

00:43:14.860 --> 00:43:19.060
with respect to brute
forcing, the computation,

00:43:19.060 --> 00:43:22.500
keep in mind that you have to
carry out this optimization

00:43:22.500 --> 00:43:25.060
problem whereby you
are trying to find

00:43:25.060 --> 00:43:29.740
the optimal control at time
k that optimizes this cost

00:43:29.740 --> 00:43:31.100
function.

00:43:31.100 --> 00:43:33.820
You have to do that
for all time steps

00:43:33.820 --> 00:43:36.700
and for all possible states.

00:43:36.700 --> 00:43:40.020
And even though time
has been discretized,

00:43:40.020 --> 00:43:42.380
technically, the state
hasn't been discretized.

00:43:42.380 --> 00:43:44.940
So in principle, this could
be a continuous quantity.

00:43:44.940 --> 00:43:47.980
So you cannot even run this
recursion because you would have

00:43:47.980 --> 00:43:49.940
infinitely many of
these recursions.

00:43:49.940 --> 00:43:52.340
Or even if you
discretize it, you

00:43:52.340 --> 00:43:56.860
might have a very large number
of possible state combinations.

00:43:56.860 --> 00:43:58.770
Let me clarify
this a little bit.

00:44:10.550 --> 00:44:13.470
Say that your state
is one-dimensional.

00:44:13.470 --> 00:44:16.790
It belongs to the
interval 0, 1, and you

00:44:16.790 --> 00:44:24.270
are discretizing with 0.1 step.

00:44:28.734 --> 00:44:34.850
And say that the time
is also between 0, 1.

00:44:34.850 --> 00:44:38.750
And you also discretize
it with a 0.1 step.

00:44:38.750 --> 00:44:40.832
So how many dynamic
programming recursions,

00:44:40.832 --> 00:44:42.790
meaning how many of those
optimization problems

00:44:42.790 --> 00:44:44.950
you have to solve?

00:44:44.950 --> 00:44:48.830
Per step, you have 10 for each
possible value of the state.

00:44:48.830 --> 00:44:52.870
But then you have a 10 time
steps, so you have 100.

00:44:52.870 --> 00:44:56.510
So you have 10 times 10.

00:44:56.510 --> 00:44:59.190
Now, let's assume
time stays the same,

00:44:59.190 --> 00:45:03.510
but let's assume that now
we go into the setting

00:45:03.510 --> 00:45:06.410
whereby x is two-dimensional.

00:45:09.590 --> 00:45:16.630
So for example, position and
velocity of a double integrator.

00:45:16.630 --> 00:45:18.510
How many dynamic
programming recursions

00:45:18.510 --> 00:45:20.750
you have to carry out?

00:45:20.750 --> 00:45:24.430
For each time step, how many
possible combinations of states

00:45:24.430 --> 00:45:26.030
do you have?

00:45:26.030 --> 00:45:27.230
100.

00:45:27.230 --> 00:45:27.890
That's right.

00:45:27.890 --> 00:45:28.890
So you have 100.

00:45:28.890 --> 00:45:34.750
So now, you have 10, which is
the number of steps times 100.

00:45:34.750 --> 00:45:38.810
So now, let's assume that
it's three-dimensional.

00:45:49.110 --> 00:45:57.000
So now, x is 0, 1,
cross 0, 1, cross 0, 1.

00:45:57.000 --> 00:45:59.860
So basically, you are optimizing
over three variables, x, y,

00:45:59.860 --> 00:46:01.200
and z.

00:46:01.200 --> 00:46:03.460
So now, you have 10
time steps as before,

00:46:03.460 --> 00:46:07.760
but now you have 10
times 10 times 10.

00:46:07.760 --> 00:46:09.920
So basically, your
dynamic recursions

00:46:09.920 --> 00:46:14.360
are scale with 10 to
the power of d, where d

00:46:14.360 --> 00:46:16.200
is the dimension of your state.

00:46:16.200 --> 00:46:20.040
So you have an
exponential scaling.

00:46:20.040 --> 00:46:21.840
So even though
dynamic programming,

00:46:21.840 --> 00:46:24.520
and you can show
this more formally,

00:46:24.520 --> 00:46:26.960
dramatically decrease the
amount of computations

00:46:26.960 --> 00:46:30.340
that you have to carry
out, still in general,

00:46:30.340 --> 00:46:33.840
you have to carry out
a lot of computations,

00:46:33.840 --> 00:46:37.840
particularly in
high-dimensional state spaces.

00:46:37.840 --> 00:46:38.340
All right.

00:46:38.340 --> 00:46:39.715
So assuming that
you can do that.

00:46:39.715 --> 00:46:41.280
So two main challenges.

00:46:41.280 --> 00:46:43.360
I'm modeling one.

00:46:43.360 --> 00:46:46.680
Whatever problem you
are, it's possible

00:46:46.680 --> 00:46:49.300
that you can map it into--
not all problems, of course,

00:46:49.300 --> 00:46:51.780
can be mapped into
this formalism.

00:46:51.780 --> 00:46:54.050
But there are some problems
that a face value may not

00:46:54.050 --> 00:46:58.250
look like amenable to this
formulation, but they are.

00:46:58.250 --> 00:47:01.233
So there is a little bit of
cleverness that goes into that.

00:47:01.233 --> 00:47:02.650
And then there is,
of course, work

00:47:02.650 --> 00:47:07.730
that goes into making sure that
you can run dynamic [INAUDIBLE]

00:47:07.730 --> 00:47:09.290
at scale.

00:47:09.290 --> 00:47:13.330
So this is the topic of
so-called approximate dynamic

00:47:13.330 --> 00:47:13.990
recursion.

00:47:13.990 --> 00:47:16.330
That will also lead
into the second part

00:47:16.330 --> 00:47:18.950
of this class, referred to
as learning-based control.

00:47:21.490 --> 00:47:25.410
Now, assuming that you
can solve this problem,

00:47:25.410 --> 00:47:28.410
then you can construct
an optimal policy

00:47:28.410 --> 00:47:31.410
by essentially bookkeeping
for all possible states,

00:47:31.410 --> 00:47:34.090
associating the optimal
control that you have picked.

00:47:34.090 --> 00:47:36.930
And so you are incrementally
building the optimal control

00:47:36.930 --> 00:47:37.910
policy pi.

00:47:43.210 --> 00:47:44.390
So there is a lot here.

00:47:44.390 --> 00:47:46.530
This is a very fundamental
result in control.

00:47:46.530 --> 00:47:52.290
It's the basis of discrete time
closed loop optimal control,

00:47:52.290 --> 00:47:55.250
continuous time closed
loop optimal control,

00:47:55.250 --> 00:47:58.650
learning-based control,
and policy learning.

00:47:58.650 --> 00:48:01.090
So everything exciting
that you hear these days

00:48:01.090 --> 00:48:05.250
about policy learning, vision
language action models, all

00:48:05.250 --> 00:48:09.430
that stuff is rooted in
this equation, of course,

00:48:09.430 --> 00:48:12.530
with many generalizations.

00:48:12.530 --> 00:48:15.490
Now, if you think
about this equation,

00:48:15.490 --> 00:48:19.810
we derive it somewhat
formally by basically stating

00:48:19.810 --> 00:48:23.150
the problem, stating the
principle of optimality,

00:48:23.150 --> 00:48:28.690
and then just applying it in
order to derive this recursion.

00:48:28.690 --> 00:48:30.950
But if you think about
it, it's quite natural.

00:48:30.950 --> 00:48:35.690
So what is it saying is that
assuming that you are a given

00:48:35.690 --> 00:48:40.170
state, the optimal
thing to do right now

00:48:40.170 --> 00:48:44.410
is what minimizes the
impact of that action

00:48:44.410 --> 00:48:49.530
on your immediate costs, plus
the implication of what you do

00:48:49.530 --> 00:48:53.900
today in terms of what
will happen tomorrow,

00:48:53.900 --> 00:48:55.040
starting from tomorrow.

00:48:57.620 --> 00:49:01.220
If you think about
it, it's very natural.

00:49:01.220 --> 00:49:08.220
Why are you here today
and not on a beach?

00:49:08.220 --> 00:49:10.740
Because you are trading
off your immediate gains

00:49:10.740 --> 00:49:12.020
with future gains.

00:49:12.020 --> 00:49:20.020
Of course, going to the beach
to have a nice swim in the ocean

00:49:20.020 --> 00:49:23.980
improves your cost right
now, but has implications

00:49:23.980 --> 00:49:26.940
about your future education.

00:49:26.940 --> 00:49:29.780
So your cost to go is affected.

00:49:29.780 --> 00:49:32.380
So actually, you
are here because you

00:49:32.380 --> 00:49:36.540
are sacrificing your
immediate wealth in order

00:49:36.540 --> 00:49:38.250
to optimize your future wealth.

00:49:38.250 --> 00:49:40.500
So that's what dynamic
programming is doing, is really

00:49:40.500 --> 00:49:44.220
compromising the immediate gains
with the implication of what

00:49:44.220 --> 00:49:49.220
you do today, starting
from tomorrow.

00:49:49.220 --> 00:49:52.580
And it does so in a
mathematical derived way.

00:49:52.580 --> 00:49:54.920
It is a principle.

00:49:54.920 --> 00:49:56.170
Yeah?

00:49:56.170 --> 00:49:59.797
I'm confused about how we end up
reaching our initial condition.

00:49:59.797 --> 00:50:01.380
Because if you have
a final condition,

00:50:01.380 --> 00:50:03.760
that's really far away from
your initial condition,

00:50:03.760 --> 00:50:05.275
I don't understand how--

00:50:05.275 --> 00:50:06.900
especially at the
beginning, it doesn't

00:50:06.900 --> 00:50:09.140
seem like there's any way
the optimal solution would

00:50:09.140 --> 00:50:11.520
drive in any way towards it.

00:50:11.520 --> 00:50:13.580
Yeah, that's why
you fall backward.

00:50:13.580 --> 00:50:17.300
So you start backward and then--

00:50:17.300 --> 00:50:18.640
I see what you're saying.

00:50:18.640 --> 00:50:23.723
So when you have x0,
you might actually--

00:50:23.723 --> 00:50:25.140
you are solving
actually a problem

00:50:25.140 --> 00:50:27.780
for all possible
initial conditions.

00:50:27.780 --> 00:50:33.300
And then you give me the initial
condition you're interested in.

00:50:33.300 --> 00:50:35.140
And for that one
specifically, we

00:50:35.140 --> 00:50:37.035
find an optimal control policy.

00:50:37.035 --> 00:50:38.160
But in a way, you're right.

00:50:38.160 --> 00:50:39.740
So the dynamic
programming problem

00:50:39.740 --> 00:50:42.940
to solve the solution-- to find
the solution to the problem

00:50:42.940 --> 00:50:44.860
that you really
care about, the one

00:50:44.860 --> 00:50:49.710
with the specific initial
condition x0 equal 1 comma 3,

00:50:49.710 --> 00:50:52.910
is actually solving all
the series of problems

00:50:52.910 --> 00:50:56.790
and all the four problems for
all possible initial conditions.

00:50:56.790 --> 00:50:58.450
Or maybe you are
the last iteration.

00:50:58.450 --> 00:51:01.270
You can just be smart
and only carry out

00:51:01.270 --> 00:51:03.490
this recursion for j x0.

00:51:03.490 --> 00:51:07.630
But in general, for all
the intermediate steps,

00:51:07.630 --> 00:51:09.550
you have to solve
the third problems

00:51:09.550 --> 00:51:12.590
from all possible states that
are reachable because you don't

00:51:12.590 --> 00:51:14.390
know from the initial
condition you're

00:51:14.390 --> 00:51:17.910
interested in which intermediate
states you'll be visiting.

00:51:17.910 --> 00:51:21.270
So in that way,
it's a bit wasteful.

00:51:21.270 --> 00:51:23.710
But the good news,
though, is that at least

00:51:23.710 --> 00:51:25.790
you still save quite
a bit in computation.

00:51:25.790 --> 00:51:28.690
And then if problem data changes
or initial condition changes,

00:51:28.690 --> 00:51:33.590
you solve the problem for
all possible conditions.

00:51:33.590 --> 00:51:36.010
It seems like the
previous example,

00:51:36.010 --> 00:51:39.650
we were sort of processing
nodes in a specific order.

00:51:39.650 --> 00:51:42.190
Yeah, that's basically the
equation on there for me.

00:51:42.190 --> 00:51:44.830
So what I have not shown--

00:51:44.830 --> 00:51:46.130
and I'll provide a reference.

00:51:46.130 --> 00:51:49.260
It's a bit convoluted,
but not to convoluted.

00:51:49.260 --> 00:51:52.000
What I did was a bit ad
hoc, but you can actually

00:51:52.000 --> 00:51:54.440
map the shortest path
problem computation exactly

00:51:54.440 --> 00:51:59.560
in this format, and I'll
post the definition covers.

00:51:59.560 --> 00:52:04.040
So, in general, do you also
need to determine some type

00:52:04.040 --> 00:52:05.920
of ordering the process status?

00:52:05.920 --> 00:52:06.420
Yeah.

00:52:06.420 --> 00:52:09.560
Because you need to put
it in that formalism.

00:52:09.560 --> 00:52:12.680
And the ordering
there is the number

00:52:12.680 --> 00:52:15.580
of hops we are optimizing over.

00:52:15.580 --> 00:52:17.560
So we first optimize
over paths that

00:52:17.560 --> 00:52:21.760
reach the final
destination in one hop.

00:52:21.760 --> 00:52:23.440
Then we optimize
over paths, reach

00:52:23.440 --> 00:52:25.440
the final destination
in two hops.

00:52:25.440 --> 00:52:29.240
We then optimize, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:52:29.240 --> 00:52:33.120
So in the case, the number of
hops is the number of stages.

00:52:33.120 --> 00:52:35.300
OK.

00:52:35.300 --> 00:52:36.340
So a few comments.

00:52:36.340 --> 00:52:38.738
So I already talked about
the discretization aspect.

00:52:38.738 --> 00:52:40.780
So that's basically how
we formulate the problem.

00:52:40.780 --> 00:52:42.822
We went from differential
equations or difference

00:52:42.822 --> 00:52:43.520
equations.

00:52:43.520 --> 00:52:46.600
We talked about how dynamic
programming recursion

00:52:46.600 --> 00:52:50.260
has to be carried out for each
possible value of the state.

00:52:50.260 --> 00:52:53.742
If the state is continuous,
then we have to quantize it.

00:52:53.742 --> 00:52:55.200
Then programming
actually is trying

00:52:55.200 --> 00:52:58.080
to find a global minimum
for each dynamic programming

00:52:58.080 --> 00:52:58.660
recursion.

00:52:58.660 --> 00:53:02.280
So it's going to give you the
globally optimal solution.

00:53:02.280 --> 00:53:04.120
If there are
constraints, that is--

00:53:04.120 --> 00:53:06.880
typically, constraints make the
optimization problem much harder

00:53:06.880 --> 00:53:11.920
as you saw with Dirac methods
and Lagrange optimization.

00:53:11.920 --> 00:53:13.560
But in this case,
constraints actually

00:53:13.560 --> 00:53:16.000
make the problem
simpler because they

00:53:16.000 --> 00:53:18.060
reduce the number of actions.

00:53:18.060 --> 00:53:20.560
Roughly speaking, you
have to optimize over.

00:53:20.560 --> 00:53:23.760
So you have less
things to consider.

00:53:23.760 --> 00:53:27.000
It gives you a solution
in closed loop form,

00:53:27.000 --> 00:53:29.415
but it is affected by the
curse of dimensionality.

00:53:29.415 --> 00:53:31.040
What the curse of
dimensionality refers

00:53:31.040 --> 00:53:35.360
to is the exponential scaling
of the number of computations

00:53:35.360 --> 00:53:38.100
needed, whereas the
dimension of the state space.

00:53:42.480 --> 00:53:44.490
I have a question on
the transfer of value,

00:53:44.490 --> 00:53:47.810
where you said that it's only
the tail of some path that

00:53:47.810 --> 00:53:50.370
is like the shortest path.

00:53:50.370 --> 00:53:52.670
Isn't it true that if
that's the global minimum,

00:53:52.670 --> 00:53:57.810
that any path, regardless of
whether it's the tail end path,

00:53:57.810 --> 00:53:59.443
is also the shortest path here?

00:53:59.443 --> 00:54:01.610
Yes, I mean, but that's the
principle of optimality.

00:54:01.610 --> 00:54:02.570
Yeah, absolutely.

00:54:02.570 --> 00:54:05.830
Every tail path is
going to be optimal.

00:54:05.830 --> 00:54:07.850
Yes.

00:54:07.850 --> 00:54:11.610
So we started from the
optimal problem formulation.

00:54:11.610 --> 00:54:13.610
We derived that the
most essential structure

00:54:13.610 --> 00:54:16.530
that this problem has referred
to as a principle optimality.

00:54:16.530 --> 00:54:18.450
We argue how leveraging
the structure

00:54:18.450 --> 00:54:22.450
we can find an optimal algorithm
to solve these problems.

00:54:22.450 --> 00:54:24.770
This algorithm has a quirk.

00:54:24.770 --> 00:54:29.290
The quirk is that it involves
doing computation backward

00:54:29.290 --> 00:54:29.990
in time.

00:54:32.970 --> 00:54:41.010
And there is a nice quote from
a philosopher that is loosely--

00:54:41.010 --> 00:54:42.850
I mean, actually,
this philosopher

00:54:42.850 --> 00:54:46.450
lived before Bellman
that introduced

00:54:46.450 --> 00:54:49.170
the optimality
principle, but it's

00:54:49.170 --> 00:54:53.430
kind of nice
philosophical association.

00:54:53.430 --> 00:54:54.730
Let's put it this way.

00:54:54.730 --> 00:55:02.070
That life has to
be lived forward.

00:55:05.730 --> 00:55:08.770
That's obvious, because
time goes forward in time.

00:55:08.770 --> 00:55:21.972
But it can only be
understood backward.

00:55:21.972 --> 00:55:23.430
I don't know how
much you resonate,

00:55:23.430 --> 00:55:26.610
but sometimes you look at
where you are and you see,

00:55:26.610 --> 00:55:29.770
I didn't realize that decision
that I made four years ago

00:55:29.770 --> 00:55:31.570
would have led me here.

00:55:31.570 --> 00:55:34.490
So that's how I understand
things backward.

00:55:34.490 --> 00:55:35.170
I don't know.

00:55:35.170 --> 00:55:36.630
It reminds me of this quote.

00:55:36.630 --> 00:55:39.770
It's up to you if
you resonate or not.

00:55:39.770 --> 00:55:42.980
One thing.

00:55:42.980 --> 00:55:47.700
For your exam,
very likely that I

00:55:47.700 --> 00:55:52.260
will ask you to compute one
step of a dynamic programming

00:55:52.260 --> 00:55:55.422
recursion to make sure that
you understand what to do.

00:55:55.422 --> 00:55:56.880
It's going to be
relatively simple.

00:55:56.880 --> 00:55:58.000
It will take five minutes.

00:55:58.000 --> 00:56:02.180
What I'm saying is make
sure that you internalize

00:56:02.180 --> 00:56:04.340
what one step of a
dynamic progression

00:56:04.340 --> 00:56:05.980
algorithm it entails.

00:56:05.980 --> 00:56:07.380
OK.

00:56:07.380 --> 00:56:11.300
What is it about this dynamic
programming approach that

00:56:11.300 --> 00:56:14.380
makes it closed loop,
as opposed to what

00:56:14.380 --> 00:56:17.620
we were learning last week?

00:56:17.620 --> 00:56:23.620
Yes, that now we
are solving problems

00:56:23.620 --> 00:56:28.540
for all possible tail problems
from all possible states

00:56:28.540 --> 00:56:29.860
we could reach.

00:56:29.860 --> 00:56:31.860
That's a completely
different mindset

00:56:31.860 --> 00:56:34.220
for what we were doing before.

00:56:34.220 --> 00:56:38.380
By doing so, you are basically
computing an optimal control

00:56:38.380 --> 00:56:40.240
for each possible
state you are in,

00:56:40.240 --> 00:56:43.070
which is really a definition of
a closed loop optimal control.

00:56:43.070 --> 00:56:46.470
So this different view,
different with respect

00:56:46.470 --> 00:56:50.150
to what we had before, that
allows us to compute a closed

00:56:50.150 --> 00:56:53.550
loop control policies.

00:56:53.550 --> 00:56:57.970
So a significant
shift in mindset.

00:56:57.970 --> 00:56:58.470
All right.

00:56:58.470 --> 00:57:04.670
So let's wrap up this
lecture by solving

00:57:04.670 --> 00:57:07.390
a relatively simple, but
extremely foundational

00:57:07.390 --> 00:57:08.870
optimal control
problem, which is

00:57:08.870 --> 00:57:14.510
referred to as LQR, linear
quadratic regulator.

00:57:14.510 --> 00:57:18.110
So the LQR problem
is a specific class

00:57:18.110 --> 00:57:22.430
of discrete time optimal control
problem defined as follows.

00:57:22.430 --> 00:57:24.450
Our dynamics are linear.

00:57:24.450 --> 00:57:29.370
So xk plus 1 is equal to
axk plus buk x to the state.

00:57:29.370 --> 00:57:30.270
Use the control.

00:57:30.270 --> 00:57:34.230
A is a given matrix,
matrix of numbers.

00:57:34.230 --> 00:57:36.130
B is also a given matrix.

00:57:36.130 --> 00:57:37.650
It does not depend on the state.

00:57:37.650 --> 00:57:41.470
It does not depend on control.

00:57:41.470 --> 00:57:43.090
This referred to as
the state matrix.

00:57:43.090 --> 00:57:44.870
This is the input matrix.

00:57:44.870 --> 00:57:47.330
The cost is additive
and is quadratic.

00:57:47.330 --> 00:57:49.910
So we have a
terminal cost, which

00:57:49.910 --> 00:57:53.310
is a quadratic form in terms
of the terminal state x of n

00:57:53.310 --> 00:57:56.590
And then the stage-wise
cost is quadratic too.

00:57:56.590 --> 00:57:59.070
It's a quadratic in
terms of the state

00:57:59.070 --> 00:58:02.350
and it's a quadratic in
terms of the control.

00:58:02.350 --> 00:58:05.350
U squared, basically,
models how unhappy

00:58:05.350 --> 00:58:07.910
you are about using control u.

00:58:07.910 --> 00:58:13.250
X square is basically
telling us how unhappy

00:58:13.250 --> 00:58:16.410
you are about being in state x.

00:58:16.410 --> 00:58:18.310
Since you are
squaring the state,

00:58:18.310 --> 00:58:23.510
you can see how here
the objective-- what

00:58:23.510 --> 00:58:26.070
is the objective
essentially modeling

00:58:26.070 --> 00:58:28.110
is that you want
to go to the origin

00:58:28.110 --> 00:58:32.230
where the state x is equal to 0,
so that you don't pay any cost,

00:58:32.230 --> 00:58:36.670
and you want to do so with
a control u that minimizes

00:58:36.670 --> 00:58:38.960
the sum of the u squares.

00:58:38.960 --> 00:58:42.887
So you want to basically
minimize energy.

00:58:42.887 --> 00:58:45.220
And of course, there is a
little bit of a conflict here.

00:58:45.220 --> 00:58:49.480
Maybe with larger u, you might
get to the origin quicker,

00:58:49.480 --> 00:58:52.160
but then you have a
bigger control effort.

00:58:52.160 --> 00:58:55.600
But then if you
make smaller steps,

00:58:55.600 --> 00:58:59.600
you'll eventually get to the
origin and you pay less control,

00:58:59.600 --> 00:59:01.600
but you are very unhappy
for a lot of steps

00:59:01.600 --> 00:59:03.900
because you are far from the
origin for a lot of steps.

00:59:03.900 --> 00:59:05.928
So basically, it's trying
to find this balance.

00:59:05.928 --> 00:59:07.720
This problem is referred
to as a regulator,

00:59:07.720 --> 00:59:09.137
because essentially,
it's modeling

00:59:09.137 --> 00:59:14.800
the problem of getting to
the origin with minimum cost.

00:59:14.800 --> 00:59:16.900
Now, for this quadratic
cost to make sense,

00:59:16.900 --> 00:59:22.160
we are requiring that h, q and
r are positive semi-definite so

00:59:22.160 --> 00:59:25.960
that these are actually
non-negative terms.

00:59:25.960 --> 00:59:27.840
As we will see
later, we are also

00:59:27.840 --> 00:59:33.240
strengthening the condition
for r to be positive definite.

00:59:33.240 --> 00:59:34.820
So this is the control penalty.

00:59:34.820 --> 00:59:36.180
This is the state penalty.

00:59:36.180 --> 00:59:40.960
And this is the terminal
state penalty matrix.

00:59:40.960 --> 00:59:44.600
Does the problem make sense?

00:59:44.600 --> 00:59:51.640
I stated it doesn't seem to be
particularly useful problem.

00:59:51.640 --> 00:59:54.340
I mean, we want to
get to the origin.

00:59:54.340 --> 00:59:58.120
But in general, who cares
about getting to the origin?

00:59:58.120 --> 01:00:00.680
As we will see, actually,
this problem formulation

01:00:00.680 --> 01:00:04.040
is extremely useful and popular.

01:00:04.040 --> 01:00:07.840
It can readily be used in the
context of trajectory tracking

01:00:07.840 --> 01:00:11.080
problems, as we will
see on Wednesday,

01:00:11.080 --> 01:00:17.400
and also, as a useful algorithm
to even solve a nonlinear

01:00:17.400 --> 01:00:22.400
optimization problem through
successive linearizations.

01:00:22.400 --> 01:00:24.460
So even in the most
complicated, say,

01:00:24.460 --> 01:00:26.520
end-to-end models for
autonomous driving,

01:00:26.520 --> 01:00:29.120
chances are that
you're tracking--

01:00:29.120 --> 01:00:31.820
where you put vision language
action models, forward models,

01:00:31.820 --> 01:00:33.400
foundation models,
what have you,

01:00:33.400 --> 01:00:36.450
chances are that your
trajectory tracking algorithm

01:00:36.450 --> 01:00:39.130
is a somewhat LQR derived.

01:00:39.130 --> 01:00:42.050
So there's an important concept.

01:00:42.050 --> 01:00:44.190
So we're looking at this
problem for two reasons.

01:00:44.190 --> 01:00:46.350
One because it's an
important class of problems,

01:00:46.350 --> 01:00:48.610
especially as we will
discuss on Wednesday.

01:00:48.610 --> 01:00:50.650
But two, it's also
one example for me

01:00:50.650 --> 01:00:54.410
to showcase dynamic programming.

01:00:54.410 --> 01:00:55.650
Why?

01:00:55.650 --> 01:00:59.170
We are exactly in the setting
of dynamic programming.

01:00:59.170 --> 01:01:03.090
We have discrete
time equation, and we

01:01:03.090 --> 01:01:06.810
have a cost that
is additive, which

01:01:06.810 --> 01:01:09.350
means we can solve the problem
with dynamic programming.

01:01:09.350 --> 01:01:10.490
How does it work?

01:01:10.490 --> 01:01:12.370
Well, there is not
much cleverness

01:01:12.370 --> 01:01:14.130
that goes into the solution.

01:01:14.130 --> 01:01:18.730
You start with a boundary
condition, basically the cost

01:01:18.730 --> 01:01:20.010
at the end.

01:01:20.010 --> 01:01:23.090
So if you're in state
x of n, the cost

01:01:23.090 --> 01:01:28.450
is simply one-half xn
transpose h x of n.

01:01:28.450 --> 01:01:31.810
And also let me denote
as a definition,

01:01:31.810 --> 01:01:37.360
discuss us with one
ounce xn transpose pn xn.

01:01:37.360 --> 01:01:40.900
So basically, I'm
referring to h as pn.

01:01:40.900 --> 01:01:42.480
This will become clearer later.

01:01:42.480 --> 01:01:43.960
For now, just remember.

01:01:46.240 --> 01:01:48.740
Of course, we want to solve the
problem from the perspective

01:01:48.740 --> 01:01:49.620
of x minus 0.

01:01:49.620 --> 01:01:52.240
But to do so, we have to
run dynamic programming.

01:01:52.240 --> 01:01:55.320
So we start by going
one step backward.

01:01:55.320 --> 01:01:58.820
So now, we say, for all
possible states that

01:01:58.820 --> 01:02:01.100
are reachable at
time n minus 1, I

01:02:01.100 --> 01:02:04.820
have no clue which
state the optimal state

01:02:04.820 --> 01:02:06.160
trajectory will go through.

01:02:06.160 --> 01:02:09.060
So I have to consider
all possible ones that

01:02:09.060 --> 01:02:11.420
are reachable from x0.

01:02:11.420 --> 01:02:14.420
So for each one of them--

01:02:14.420 --> 01:02:17.740
let's call each one
of them xn minus 1.

01:02:17.740 --> 01:02:21.020
So if this is one-dimensional,
and between 0 and 1

01:02:21.020 --> 01:02:23.360
discretizing with step
0, 1, I will do it

01:02:23.360 --> 01:02:24.860
for state xn equals 0.

01:02:24.860 --> 01:02:28.660
Xn is equal to 0.1, 0.2,
0.3, all the way to 10.

01:02:28.660 --> 01:02:31.220
Well, for each
one of them, I try

01:02:31.220 --> 01:02:35.540
to find the control
at time n minus 1 that

01:02:35.540 --> 01:02:41.860
minimizes the immediate cost,
which is the quadratic in xn

01:02:41.860 --> 01:02:44.900
minus 1 and the
quadratic in un minus 1,

01:02:44.900 --> 01:02:47.860
and the optimal cost
to go, so j star

01:02:47.860 --> 01:02:51.260
n x of n, which in this
case is simply xnhxn.

01:02:55.220 --> 01:02:59.500
The cost of the problem for
the state I transition into,

01:02:59.500 --> 01:03:02.640
which will be the problem
at the next stage.

01:03:05.460 --> 01:03:06.780
OK.

01:03:06.780 --> 01:03:17.060
So for x of n, it's simply
given by an minus 1, xn minus 1

01:03:17.060 --> 01:03:22.660
plus bn minus 1, un minus 1.

01:03:22.660 --> 01:03:30.510
So I plug this
relationship in here.

01:03:30.510 --> 01:03:36.950
And as you can see, now
this optimization problem

01:03:36.950 --> 01:03:40.870
is just an optimization
problem with respect

01:03:40.870 --> 01:03:43.270
to the variable un minus 1.

01:03:43.270 --> 01:03:46.710
Xn minus 1 is fixed because
for each dynamic programming

01:03:46.710 --> 01:03:51.630
recursion, you are doing it for
a specific value of xn minus 1.

01:03:51.630 --> 01:03:57.890
Qrab is o, and h is all
problem data given to you.

01:03:57.890 --> 01:04:01.710
The only quantity that is
available here is un minus 1.

01:04:01.710 --> 01:04:03.970
But this is a problem that
is very simple to solve.

01:04:03.970 --> 01:04:07.430
It's just a quadratic
in un minus 1.

01:04:07.430 --> 01:04:12.170
We have some quadratic
term coming from here.

01:04:12.170 --> 01:04:13.290
So what is this quantity?

01:04:13.290 --> 01:04:28.270
So we have an minus 1, xn
minus 1 plus bn minus 1,

01:04:28.270 --> 01:04:35.390
un minus 1 transpose haxn
xn minus 1, n minus 1

01:04:35.390 --> 01:04:40.550
plus bn minus 1, un minus 1.

01:04:40.550 --> 01:04:43.790
So this is equal to--

01:04:43.790 --> 01:04:46.290
well, we have the
quadratic in un minus 1.

01:04:46.290 --> 01:04:54.310
So we have un minus 1
transpose bn minus 1 transpose

01:04:54.310 --> 01:04:57.990
hbn minus 1, un minus 1.

01:04:57.990 --> 01:05:00.490
So that's the quadratic
in un minus 1.

01:05:00.490 --> 01:05:02.890
Then we have the
quadratic in xn minus 1.

01:05:02.890 --> 01:05:11.830
So this is plus xn minus 1
transpose an minus 1 transpose

01:05:11.830 --> 01:05:16.150
han minus 1, xn minus 1.

01:05:16.150 --> 01:05:19.010
And then we have
the cross terms.

01:05:19.010 --> 01:05:25.470
So we have a plus un minus
1 transpose, bn minus 1

01:05:25.470 --> 01:05:37.560
transpose, haxn minus 1 plus
the other term, plus xn minus 1

01:05:37.560 --> 01:05:47.320
transpose an minus 1 transpose
hbn minus 1, un minus 1.

01:05:47.320 --> 01:05:53.360
So you have a quadratic
in u, linear in u,

01:05:53.360 --> 01:05:56.200
and then no dependency in u.

01:05:56.200 --> 01:05:59.400
So now to find the minimum, you
take the derivative with respect

01:05:59.400 --> 01:06:01.760
to un minus 1.

01:06:01.760 --> 01:06:08.700
So we also have the
quadratic here in u.

01:06:08.700 --> 01:06:10.917
And then we have no
dependency u here.

01:06:10.917 --> 01:06:13.000
So when you take the
derivative with respect to u,

01:06:13.000 --> 01:06:14.400
this goes away.

01:06:14.400 --> 01:06:15.340
This is a quadratic.

01:06:15.340 --> 01:06:17.760
So the derivative with
respect to u is simply ru.

01:06:20.600 --> 01:06:23.380
Remember, there is a one-half
that's multiplying all of that.

01:06:26.730 --> 01:06:29.590
And then I leave this
as an exercise for you.

01:06:29.590 --> 01:06:32.190
But then you take a derivative.

01:06:32.190 --> 01:06:35.050
You take derivative of
all this stuff here.

01:06:35.050 --> 01:06:37.770
So here, you're left with a
term that is linear into u

01:06:37.770 --> 01:06:44.210
b transpose hbu,
b transpose hbu.

01:06:44.210 --> 01:06:46.530
And then you take the
derivatives with respect

01:06:46.530 --> 01:06:50.170
to determinate linear u,
this guy and this guy.

01:06:50.170 --> 01:06:51.755
And then you sum them up.

01:06:51.755 --> 01:06:53.130
Remember that you
have a one-half

01:06:53.130 --> 01:06:54.270
in front of all this stuff.

01:06:54.270 --> 01:06:58.678
And what you get is
b transpose ha xn.

01:06:58.678 --> 01:07:00.470
I leave it to you the
detailed computation.

01:07:00.470 --> 01:07:02.102
Super easy.

01:07:02.102 --> 01:07:04.310
And then that's a necessary
condition for optimality.

01:07:04.310 --> 01:07:06.050
You get to the second
order derivative

01:07:06.050 --> 01:07:08.050
to find a sufficient one.

01:07:08.050 --> 01:07:11.890
So taking another round
derivatives with respect to u,

01:07:11.890 --> 01:07:14.810
the derivative of this term
with respect to u is r.

01:07:14.810 --> 01:07:17.690
The derivative of all this
term with respect to u

01:07:17.690 --> 01:07:19.210
is b transpose hb.

01:07:23.130 --> 01:07:25.570
And this stuff is
positive definite

01:07:25.570 --> 01:07:28.310
because we assume that
r is positive definite.

01:07:43.050 --> 01:07:45.930
So what is the optimal u?

01:07:45.930 --> 01:07:47.370
Well, it's the
one that satisfies

01:07:47.370 --> 01:07:48.870
the necessary
optimality conditions.

01:07:48.870 --> 01:07:52.130
So you solve for the first
equation in terms of u.

01:07:52.130 --> 01:07:54.230
So you grouped
everything in terms of u,

01:07:54.230 --> 01:07:56.203
and then you take the inverse.

01:07:56.203 --> 01:07:58.370
So I'm not going to throw
all the steps because this

01:07:58.370 --> 01:07:59.890
is really simple algebra.

01:07:59.890 --> 01:08:03.210
The inverse exists because
r is positive definite.

01:08:03.210 --> 01:08:05.370
So this entire object
is positive definite,

01:08:05.370 --> 01:08:10.070
which means the determinant is
not 0, you can take the inverse.

01:08:10.070 --> 01:08:10.570
OK.

01:08:10.570 --> 01:08:14.850
So we have found the
optimal solution.

01:08:14.850 --> 01:08:16.850
And look how that's--

01:08:16.850 --> 01:08:19.569
the interesting part here
that this optimal solution

01:08:19.569 --> 01:08:25.260
is going to be given by an ugly
multiplication of matrices.

01:08:25.260 --> 01:08:27.220
But this is system
data, stuff that is

01:08:27.220 --> 01:08:29.580
given to you times the state.

01:08:29.580 --> 01:08:34.220
So actually, the optimal
control is the linear feedback

01:08:34.220 --> 01:08:35.300
on the state.

01:08:35.300 --> 01:08:39.500
It's just some matrix f times x.

01:08:39.500 --> 01:08:40.439
Great.

01:08:40.439 --> 01:08:41.880
It makes a lot of sense.

01:08:41.880 --> 01:08:44.220
And of course, there
are deeper relationship

01:08:44.220 --> 01:08:46.660
with the linear controls.

01:08:46.660 --> 01:08:50.180
So fn minus 1 is defined
as the multiplication

01:08:50.180 --> 01:08:51.439
of all these matrices.

01:08:54.020 --> 01:08:55.790
OK.

01:08:55.790 --> 01:08:59.060
But then what is the cost
for the tail problems?

01:08:59.060 --> 01:09:02.420
So we plug back in the
optimal control input

01:09:02.420 --> 01:09:05.939
as we compute it into the cost.

01:09:05.939 --> 01:09:14.580
So now, we plug u equal
f of x back into here.

01:09:14.580 --> 01:09:17.420
So this will be x
transpose n minus 1,

01:09:17.420 --> 01:09:21.660
f minus 1 transpose r
fn minus 1, xn minus 1.

01:09:21.660 --> 01:09:23.160
So you plug all that in.

01:09:26.220 --> 01:09:28.920
And again, I'm not going
to go through the steps.

01:09:28.920 --> 01:09:30.899
If you don't trust me,
you can go through them.

01:09:30.899 --> 01:09:36.340
But basically, what I get
is a quadratic form in terms

01:09:36.340 --> 01:09:38.040
of the state xn minus 1.

01:09:41.819 --> 01:09:45.939
Just with this exercise,
you pick u equal fx

01:09:45.939 --> 01:09:47.800
as the optimal solution.

01:09:47.800 --> 01:09:52.819
Go back into the cost
function with that form of u,

01:09:52.819 --> 01:09:57.220
and what you get that the
cost is quadratic in x.

01:09:57.220 --> 01:10:00.540
So then you start
seeing some pattern.

01:10:00.540 --> 01:10:05.380
We started with quadratic
cost xn minus 1, hxn minus 1.

01:10:05.380 --> 01:10:07.460
Actually, I said no.

01:10:07.460 --> 01:10:10.020
For terminology,
let's call that hpn.

01:10:10.020 --> 01:10:13.660
So we started with the cost
that was quadratic xn minus 1

01:10:13.660 --> 01:10:17.060
transpose pn xn minus 1.

01:10:17.060 --> 01:10:20.710
I found that for going
one step backward,

01:10:20.710 --> 01:10:23.590
the optimal control is
linear feedback on the state,

01:10:23.590 --> 01:10:27.550
and the optimal cost
for the tail problems

01:10:27.550 --> 01:10:30.950
is quadratic in the state.

01:10:30.950 --> 01:10:34.010
So what happens if you
go one step backward,

01:10:34.010 --> 01:10:36.550
you start with a quadratic
optimal cost to go,

01:10:36.550 --> 01:10:39.630
and you ended up with a
quadratic optimal cost

01:10:39.630 --> 01:10:40.910
for the tail.

01:10:40.910 --> 01:10:43.657
So if you go one
step backward, what

01:10:43.657 --> 01:10:45.490
do you think is going
to happen to the cost?

01:10:45.490 --> 01:10:48.390
Is it going to be
quadratic, cubic?

01:10:48.390 --> 01:10:49.170
We don't know.

01:10:51.910 --> 01:10:54.688
Mathematically, the problem
has the exact same structure.

01:10:54.688 --> 01:10:57.230
So you're going to be faced with
exactly the same problem you

01:10:57.230 --> 01:10:58.150
are formulating.

01:10:58.150 --> 01:11:01.070
Just that now instead of having
n minus 1, the subscript,

01:11:01.070 --> 01:11:03.270
you have n minus 2.

01:11:03.270 --> 01:11:07.710
Which essentially means we
found a very nice pattern

01:11:07.710 --> 01:11:10.170
where we can solve this
problem by recursions.

01:11:10.170 --> 01:11:14.130
But instead of solving
an optimization problem

01:11:14.130 --> 01:11:18.510
at each step, you just
solve a recursive equation

01:11:18.510 --> 01:11:22.680
on the matrices fk and pk.

01:11:22.680 --> 01:11:27.760
So you start from a boundary
condition setting pn equal h.

01:11:27.760 --> 01:11:32.920
Then you set the gain for
the optimal control f of k

01:11:32.920 --> 01:11:34.620
equal to all the stuff.

01:11:34.620 --> 01:11:37.840
This is stuff that depends
on problem data or stuff that

01:11:37.840 --> 01:11:41.600
has already been computed
at the previous step.

01:11:41.600 --> 01:11:44.920
Given the gain, then
you compute the cost

01:11:44.920 --> 01:11:49.360
from the perspective of
time k by updating pk

01:11:49.360 --> 01:11:53.400
according to this relatively
ugly, but simple to implement

01:11:53.400 --> 01:11:55.880
equation whereby you are
just multiplying the problem

01:11:55.880 --> 01:11:59.560
data and stuff that has
been already computed.

01:11:59.560 --> 01:12:01.540
And then you go
one step backward.

01:12:01.540 --> 01:12:03.520
Does it make sense?

01:12:03.520 --> 01:12:06.600
So it's just one lucky case
whereby the dynamic programming

01:12:06.600 --> 01:12:12.800
recursion boils down to
recursion on some matrices.

01:12:12.800 --> 01:12:16.720
These equations actually are
very important in control

01:12:16.720 --> 01:12:21.140
and are referred to
as a Riccati equation.

01:12:21.140 --> 01:12:25.080
Riccati was an
Italian mathematician.

01:12:25.080 --> 01:12:29.700
So, as you know, I'm
originally from Italy.

01:12:29.700 --> 01:12:32.860
So I do care that you
spell Riccati correctly.

01:12:32.860 --> 01:12:39.865
So, Riccati is two
c's here and 1 t.

01:12:39.865 --> 01:12:41.240
For whatever
reason, I don't know

01:12:41.240 --> 01:12:43.620
why in the United States,
sometimes-- oftentimes,

01:12:43.620 --> 01:12:51.040
this is referred to as
either Riccatti or Ricatti.

01:12:51.040 --> 01:12:54.160
By the way, Riccati in
Italian means blackmailing.

01:12:54.160 --> 01:12:56.920
So not this one, not
this one, this one.

01:12:56.920 --> 01:12:59.280
OK, Riccati, two c, 1 t.

01:12:59.280 --> 01:13:00.960
Great.

01:13:00.960 --> 01:13:03.460
So bottom line here.

01:13:03.460 --> 01:13:08.200
So we discussed the foundations
of a closed loop optimal

01:13:08.200 --> 01:13:09.360
control.

01:13:09.360 --> 01:13:12.662
We discussed how by using
fundamental structure

01:13:12.662 --> 01:13:14.120
in our problem
formulation referred

01:13:14.120 --> 01:13:15.740
to as the principle
of optimality,

01:13:15.740 --> 01:13:18.250
we can derive an actionable
algorithm referred

01:13:18.250 --> 01:13:19.750
to as dynamic programming.

01:13:19.750 --> 01:13:21.610
Dynamic programming
is purely procedural.

01:13:21.610 --> 01:13:24.970
It means that once you're
given the problem formulation,

01:13:24.970 --> 01:13:27.890
you just write down in
Python, these days, maybe

01:13:27.890 --> 01:13:31.050
use Claude to write
your recursion.

01:13:31.050 --> 01:13:32.270
That's the easy part.

01:13:32.270 --> 01:13:34.990
The hard part is on
the modeling side,

01:13:34.990 --> 01:13:37.090
making sure that your
problem is efficiently

01:13:37.090 --> 01:13:41.010
formulated in the same
formalism of the optimal control

01:13:41.010 --> 01:13:42.010
formulation.

01:13:42.010 --> 01:13:44.453
I'll share a pager
that shows you

01:13:44.453 --> 01:13:46.370
how to do that for the
shortest path problems.

01:13:46.370 --> 01:13:48.130
There were a couple
of questions today.

01:13:48.130 --> 01:13:51.010
And of course, doing
dynamic programming at scale

01:13:51.010 --> 01:13:52.610
requires a number
of approximations

01:13:52.610 --> 01:13:54.910
that we will discuss
later on in this class.

01:13:54.910 --> 01:13:57.130
That's the hard part
of dynamic programming.

01:13:57.130 --> 01:14:00.090
We look at a special case of
dynamic programming, which

01:14:00.090 --> 01:14:03.410
is dynamic programming
applied to LQR.

01:14:03.410 --> 01:14:06.190
LQR is a problem of
its own interest,

01:14:06.190 --> 01:14:08.570
as we will see,
particularly on Wednesday,

01:14:08.570 --> 01:14:10.570
it has a number of very
useful applications

01:14:10.570 --> 01:14:15.650
in robotics and
controls tracking

01:14:15.650 --> 01:14:17.230
nonlinear optimal control.

01:14:17.230 --> 01:14:19.050
And we'll see.

01:14:19.050 --> 01:14:24.490
And the beauty of LQR is
that the dynamic programming

01:14:24.490 --> 01:14:29.370
recursions simplify into
recursions about just two

01:14:29.370 --> 01:14:30.690
quantities.

01:14:30.690 --> 01:14:37.170
The gain matrix for the optimal
controller, which is provably

01:14:37.170 --> 01:14:42.290
in the linear feedback form, and
another matrix p that tells you

01:14:42.290 --> 01:14:45.810
the cost for each subproblem.

01:14:45.810 --> 01:14:48.450
And of course, LQR
was also a way for me

01:14:48.450 --> 01:14:51.370
to give an example
about carrying out

01:14:51.370 --> 01:14:53.930
the dynamic
programming recursion.

01:14:53.930 --> 01:14:54.810
Make sense?

01:14:54.810 --> 01:14:58.930
So what we're going to do next
is then-- so on Wednesday,

01:14:58.930 --> 01:15:02.850
is to see how LQR can
be applied to a number

01:15:02.850 --> 01:15:06.050
of additional problems that
are very useful, particularly

01:15:06.050 --> 01:15:07.410
in robotics.

01:15:07.410 --> 01:15:08.110
All right.

01:15:08.110 --> 01:15:10.180
See you on Wednesday.

